// chem-demo — a comprehensive demonstration of the chem package. Render with:
//   texish scripts/chem-demo.script
//
// The chem package has two halves: a small set of drawing macros for structural
// formulas (\atom, \bond, \dbond, \tbond, tuned by bondgap and doublesep), and a
// set of inline notation macros for formulas and reaction equations (\dn, \up, and
// the mhchem-style \ce). This demo exercises every one of them over real chemistry.
//
// \use{document} gives us section headings, a title block, and paragraph
// indentation; \use{chem} brings in the package itself.
\use{document}
\use{chem}

\title{Typesetting Chemistry with \mono{chem}}
\author{The texish project}
\maketitle

\section{Formula notation}

A molecular formula is just running text with the atom counts dropped and
shrunk into subscripts. The package supplies \mono{\\dn} for a subscript count
and \mono{\\up} for a superscript charge; both size themselves from the current
font through the engine's \mono{\\textsub} and \mono{\\textsup}.

\subsection{Subscripts}

Write \mono{H\\dn2 O} for water, H\dn2 O, or \mono{C\\dn6 H\\dn{12} O\\dn6}
for glucose, C\dn6 H\dn{12} O\dn6. Multi-digit counts go in braces. A few more:
sulfuric acid H\dn2 SO\dn4, ammonia NH\dn3, methane CH\dn4, and the carbonate of
calcium CaCO\dn3.

\subsection{Charges and ions}

A charge is a superscript, set with \mono{\\up}. Use a math plus or minus inside
it — \mono{Na\\up\{\$+\$\}} — so the sign is a true plus or minus glyph from the
math font rather than a hyphen. The sodium ion Na\up{$+$} and the chloride ion
Cl\up{$-$}; the doubly charged calcium Ca\up{$2+$}; and a polyatomic ion built
from a subscript and a charge together, the sulfate SO\dn4\up{$2-$} and the
phosphate PO\dn4\up{$3-$}. The hydronium and hydroxide pair: H\dn3 O\up{$+$} and
OH\up{$-$}.

\subsection{Isotopes}

An isotope carries its mass number as a leading superscript and, where useful, its
atomic number as a leading subscript. Both are just \mono{\\up} and \mono{\\dn}
written \italic{before} the symbol: \up{14}C is carbon-14, \up{235}U is the fissile
uranium isotope, and with the atomic number too, \up{14}\dn6 C and \up{238}\dn{92} U.

\section{Reaction equations}

The \mono{\\ce} macro typesets a whole reaction from compact, mhchem-style
notation. Species are separated by spaces; a leading number is a stoichiometric
coefficient, a run of letters is an element symbol (set upright), a digit run after
letters is a subscript count, \mono{+} joins species, and the arrows are
\mono{->}, \mono{<=>}, \mono{<->}, and \mono{<-}. Everything is assembled
as math markup, so \mono{+} comes out as a binary operator and the arrows as real
math glyphs centred on the text axis.

\subsection{Combustion}

\mono{\\ce} flows inline in the running text. The complete combustion of methane,
\ce{CH4 + 2 O2 -> CO2 + 2 H2O}, sits right in the sentence at the text size. So
does the combustion of propane, \ce{C3H8 + 5 O2 -> 3 CO2 + 4 H2O}, and the burning
of ethanol, \ce{C2H6O + 3 O2 -> 2 CO2 + 3 H2O}.

To set a reaction on its own centred line — and at a larger size — put \mono{\\ce}
inside \mono{\\centerline}:

\bigskip
\centerline{\font lmroman 14 regular \ce{2 C8H18 + 25 O2 -> 16 CO2 + 18 H2O}}
\bigskip

\subsection{Equilibrium}

A reversible reaction takes the equilibrium arrow \mono{<=>}, which \mono{\\ce}
renders as $\rightleftharpoons$. The Haber synthesis of ammonia is the classic
example, \ce{N2 + 3 H2 <=> 2 NH3}, as is the contact-process step toward sulfuric
acid, \ce{2 SO2 + O2 <=> 2 SO3}. A double-headed arrow \mono{<->} reads
$\leftrightarrow$, and a reverse arrow \mono{<-} reads $\leftarrow$:
\ce{2 NH3 <- N2 + 3 H2}.

\subsection{Acid--base and precipitation}

Because \mono{\\ce} cannot carry a charge through its argument, ionic equations
are written with \mono{\\dn} and \mono{\\up} directly in the running text. The
neutralization of a strong acid and base, H\up{$+$} $+$ OH\up{$-$}
$\longrightarrow$ H\dn2 O, and the dissolution of sodium chloride,
NaCl $\longrightarrow$ Na\up{$+$} $+$ Cl\up{$-$}. A precipitation, silver chloride
falling out of solution: Ag\up{$+$} $+$ Cl\up{$-$} $\longrightarrow$ AgCl.

\subsection{Redox}

A half-reaction shows electrons explicitly. The reduction of copper ions,
Cu\up{$2+$} $+$ 2 e\up{$-$} $\longrightarrow$ Cu, and the oxidation of iron,
Fe $\longrightarrow$ Fe\up{$3+$} $+$ 3 e\up{$-$}. The full neutral reaction
between zinc and hydrochloric acid is easy in \mono{\\ce}:
\ce{Zn + 2 HCl -> ZnCl2 + H2}.

\section{Structural drawings}

The drawing half of the package builds structural formulas over the picture
primitives. Name an atom and its label with \mono{\\atom}, then connect named
atoms with \mono{\\bond} (single), \mono{\\dbond} (double), or \mono{\\tbond}
(triple). Every bond is shortened at both ends by \italic{bondgap} so the atom
labels stay clear; \italic{doublesep} sets how far apart the lines of a multiple
bond run. The geometry is pure point arithmetic — no engine code.

\subsection{Atoms and single bonds: water}

\mono{\\atom} places a centred element label and names the point;
\mono{\\bond} joins two named atoms by name.

\bigskip
\centerline{\picture width:2in height:1.4in {
  \translate{72 34}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \atom{O}{(0, 44)}{O}
  \atom{Ha}{(-36, 4)}{H}
  \atom{Hb}{(36, 4)}{H}
  \bond{(O)}{(Ha)}
  \bond{(O)}{(Hb)}
}}
\bigskip

\subsection{Double bonds: carbon dioxide}

\mono{\\dbond} draws two parallel lines. Here the linear O=C=O of carbon dioxide,
both bonds double.

\bigskip
\centerline{\picture width:3in height:0.8in {
  \translate{34 30}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \atom{O1}{(0, 0)}{O}
  \atom{C}{(72, 0)}{C}
  \atom{O2}{(144, 0)}{O}
  \dbond{(O1)}{(C)}
  \dbond{(C)}{(O2)}
}}
\bigskip

\subsection{Triple bonds: acetylene}

\mono{\\tbond} draws three parallel lines. Acetylene is H--C$\equiv$C--H, a
triple bond between the carbons.

\bigskip
\centerline{\picture width:3in height:0.7in {
  \translate{20 26}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \atom{Ha}{(0, 0)}{H}
  \atom{C1}{(54, 0)}{C}
  \atom{C2}{(120, 0)}{C}
  \atom{Hb}{(174, 0)}{H}
  \bond{(Ha)}{(C1)}
  \tbond{(C1)}{(C2)}
  \bond{(C2)}{(Hb)}
}}
\bigskip

\subsection{Rings: benzene}

Coordinates make rings easy: place the carbons and hydrogens on two polar rings,
then loop the bonds, alternating single and double around the ring.

\bigskip
\centerline{\picture width:2.7in height:2.6in {
  \translate{97 94}
  \set R {46} \set HR {86}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \for\k{\range{0}{5}}{
    \set a {\calc{k*60}} \set b {\calc{(k+1)*60}}
    \if{\calc{k % 2}}
      \bond{(a:R)}{(b:R)}
    \else
      \dbond{(a:R)}{(b:R)}
    \fi
    \bond{(a:R)}{(a:HR)}
    \at anchor:center {(a:R)}{C}
    \at anchor:center {(a:HR)}{H}
  }
}}
\bigskip

\subsection{Tuning the bond style}

The clearance at each bond end and the gap of a multiple bond are the variables
\italic{bondgap} and \italic{doublesep}; reassign them to restyle every bond that
follows. Below, the same ethene C=C drawn at the default \italic{doublesep} and
then with a wider one.

\bigskip
\centerline{\picture width:3in height:0.9in {
  \translate{30 30}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \atom{C1}{(0, 0)}{C}  \atom{C2}{(72, 0)}{C}
  \dbond{(C1)}{(C2)}
  \set doublesep {5}
  \atom{C3}{(150, 0)}{C}  \atom{C4}{(222, 0)}{C}
  \dbond{(C3)}{(C4)}
}}
\bigskip

\vfill\eject
